[43] assistant: I inspected current upstream `main` at commit `cd7e606` / release `v1.1.3` (31 August 2026). No project files or changes were added to your workspace.

One important discovery: the current upstream already contains more web functionality than the brief suggests, including a persistent LAN Wi‑FiManager portal and mDNS. We should extend that rather than introduce a competing web server. [Upstream repository](https://github.com/MatixYo/ESP32-Plane-Radar)

## 1. Existing structure

- PlatformIO/Arduino project targeting `esp32-c3-devkitm-1`.
- `src/main.cpp` owns the main loop:
  - button polling
  - Wi‑Fi portal/reconnection servicing
  - ADS-B polling
  - radar redraws
- `services/wifi_setup` owns:
  - Wi‑FiManager captive portal
  - LAN configuration portal
  - mDNS at `plane-radar.local`
  - button ISR and long-press reset
- `services/radar_location` owns persisted latitude/longitude.
- `services/adsb_client` performs HTTPS requests to adsb.fi and parses aircraft data.
- `ui/radar_display`, `radar_range`, and `runway_overlay` handle rendering and range behavior.
- Hardware/display configuration is isolated under `hardware/` and `config.h`.
- CI builds a merged USB-flash image and creates tagged releases. The latest merged release image is 1,374,960 bytes. [v1.1.3 release](https://github.com/MatixYo/ESP32-Plane-Radar/releases/tag/v1.1.3)

There is currently no firmware-version constant compiled into the application. Versioning exists only in Git tags and release filenames.

## 2. Recommended location for web and OTA

Add a small `services/web_portal` module and register its routes through WiFiManager’s existing web-server callback.

That approach:

- Reuses the existing port 80 server and mDNS lifecycle.
- Avoids a second server competing with WiFiManager.
- Leaves the captive portal and radar loop intact.
